# Per-test scheduling properties, derived from measurement rather than guessed.
#
# TIMEOUT is three times the longest this test has been seen to take on CI,
# floored at 120s and capped at 3600s. The baseline has to come from CI and not
# a workstation: a test near thirteen seconds on an idle machine reaches over a
# thousand on an instrumented build on the slower half of the fleet, so local
# figures would time out CI wholesale. Most tests land on the floor, which is
# still a large multiple of what they need, so a cap firing still means the test
# is stuck rather than merely slow.
#
# PROCESSORS is measured CPU time over wall time. ctest otherwise assumes one
# core per test, lets others start alongside a test that uses several, and runs
# the machine harder than it believes. An average understates a test whose
# threads are busy only briefly, so treat these as a floor.
#
# RESOURCE_GROUPS bounds how many device-heavy tests run at once, independently
# of the parallel level. It requires ctest --resource-spec-file and is inert
# without it: ctest then applies no cap and still reports success.
#
# A test whose property is set by hand in CMakeLists.txt is absent from this
# file and keeps its own value, because a thread pool's width is something no
# measurement can see.

# A build configuration that does not register a test still has to configure:
# the enterprise-only tests are absent from a community build, and
# set_tests_properties on an unknown test fails the configure outright.
function(mg_set_test_properties name)
  if(TEST ${name})
    set_tests_properties(${name} PROPERTIES ${ARGN})
  endif()
endfunction()

